# Per-chat branch workflow

For each new chat involving project changes, create one feature branch and keep all fixes and features related to that chat on it. This repo opts in to committing and pushing completed work on that branch without a separate request.

## Before editing
1. Fetch `origin` when available. Start from the latest `origin/master`; if the remote is unavailable, use the locally known `origin/master` and disclose that it may be stale.
2. Before changing files, create and check out a branch named `cursor/<short-chat-topic>` for a Cursor chat, or `codex/<short-chat-topic>` for a Codex chat. If this chat already has a branch, keep using it for follow-up requests.
3. Stage only files related to this chat. Leave unrelated dirty files and secrets unstaged.

## After completing work in the chat
1. Commit the relevant files with a concise message and push the chat branch with `git push -u origin HEAD`.
2. Keep using the same branch for later requests in this chat. Open or update a PR when appropriate, but merge only when the user asks to merge this chat's branch.
3. Tell the user the branch name and whether it has been pushed.

## Never
- Do not `git push` directly to `master` or `main`.
- Do not force-push, skip hooks, or amend unless the user explicitly asks.
